  "query_preview": "..\nSYNOPTIC SETUP: An upper-level ridge over the central United \nStates and an Eastern-Pacific neutrally tilted longwave trough, \nwest of British Columbia/Washington State over the Pacific is \ncreating SW flow aloft over Montana. An omega blocking pattern is \npresent over CONUS which is causing the surface low advancing \ntowards our region to be delayed into Monday, perhaps into the \nevening of Monday. \nTONIGHT THROUGH TOMORROW: Currently, meridional setup has begun in\nthe region as the ridge has starting exiting eastward, ushering in \nan upper-level disturbance to our area by this evening. This \ndisturbance is associated with mid-to-low level perturbations \nwhich may cause some increased forcing, though forecast \npreciptable water content is not sufficient per current model \nguidance. PoPs should remain in the single digits throughout this \nperiod. An enhanced pressure gradient force (PGF) will form due to\nthe cold frontal passage causing breezy conditions with peak \ngusts up to 35 mph. A Lake Wind Advisory is currently in effect \nuntil Saturday at 9AM. \n\nTOMORROW NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY: Due to the current blocking\npattern, the embedded negatively-tilted shortwave trough will \ncontinue eastward as the main trough over western Montana and the \nfront passes by early Saturday morning. Frontal passage will cause\nwinds to veer W/NW and high temperatures Sunday will drop into \nthe mid to upper 60s. Post frontal passage timing remains steady.\nFurthermore, pressure gradient force (PGF) increases, leading to \nfaster winds by late Saturday afternoon.\n\nSUNDAY INTO MONDAY NIGHT: The trough axis will exit to the NE, \nleaving SW flow aloft over Montana in it's wake. Low-level \nmoisture moving through this flow coupled with isolated pockets of\nvorticity may generate rain showers in the furthest-most\nsoutheastern counties. Thunderstorms cannot be ruled out during \nthis time in the Yellowstone Valley. Currently, the chance of a \nwetting rain is possible in southeast Dawson County and to areas \nfurther east and south of that \n\nTUESDAY MORNING ONWARD: Forecast has shifted a bit since the last\nupdate. Current model guidance suggests Hurricane Polo \nmay create a conveyor belt of moisture transport into our region,\npotentially elevating PoPs. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nSurface winds in the short-term are aligning closer to baseline \nNBM rather than NBM 90th percentile. NBM90 was initially used last\nupdate, however, has become a tad too aggressive. The cold \nfrontal passage timing has also deviated from today, shifting the\npassage into the early morning hours of Saturday due to the \nblocking pattern currently in place. There is still some uncertainty\nduring this forecast update regarding possible stronger wind \ngusts overnight after the frontal passage throughout the early \nmorning hours on Saturday.\n\nFINDLEY",
